## 1. WHY THIS PROFESSION EXISTS NOW

This is no longer architectural speculation. NASA's Artemis program is explicitly building toward a sustained lunar presence — not a single mission but a continuously evolving architecture developed with industry, academia, and international partners. The scale is already defined: a moon base covering hundreds of square miles near the south pole, populated by hopping drones and lunar terrain vehicles capable of autonomous operation — assets that must land, establish, and function before any crew arrives [Space.com].

Unlike Apollo, which designed a landing site, Artemis is designing a city. A city that builds itself, runs itself, and survives itself while most of its future residents are 240,000 miles away.

The three foundational technologies driving this are 3D printing, robotics, and artificial intelligence [Earth.com]. Every professional competency in this blueprint is anchored to that triad. The Lunar City Architect is the integrating role that determines how those three technologies coexist across a spatial domain measured in hundreds of square miles, under a 1.3-second communication delay from the nearest authority that can overrule a local decision.

**Core premise:** The Lunar City Architect doesn't design buildings. They design the urban operating system that lets buildings, robots, power networks, transport corridors, and human populations co-evolve over decades — without the ability to call a plumber, send a fire truck, or restart from scratch.

