## 3. DISTINCTIVE CONSTRAINTS THAT DEFINE THE ROLE

The lunar urban environment is unlike any settlement ever planned. The constraints are not political or financial. They are physical and survival-critical, and they have no terrestrial analogues.

**Communication latency.** Earth oversight operates at a 1.3-second minimum delay, increasing to 3–20 minutes for Mars. Governance logic embedded in the city's infrastructure must function autonomously between Earth commands. The urban operating system cannot assume a human decision is one second away.

**Power geometry.** Habitats must be placed on ridge tops to access continuous solar energy. Nuclear power systems must be sited a kilometer or more away from habitation zones to manage radiation exposure. Water ice sits in permanently shadowed craters — the coldest, darkest zones on the lunar surface. These three resources pull the city in three physically incompatible directions simultaneously.

**Construction before occupation.** Three primary equipment categories are operating on the Moon before the first permanent resident arrives: excavation and transport systems, mobile 3D printing platforms, and robotic masonry assemblers [ScienceDirect]. The city is being built by machines that have never been visited by the people who will live in it.

**Autonomous decision-making agents in transit.** NASA's lunar rovers use robotic arms that can swap their own tool ends without human input, operated either remotely from Earth or autonomously by onboard systems [National Geographic]. The transport network of this city is not composed of passive vehicles following fixed routes — it is composed of semi-autonomous agents making local navigation decisions. Traffic planning must account for this.

**Failure intolerance.** Earth cities can reroute around a collapsed bridge. A badly placed power line or dust-accumulating berm on the Moon can cascade to atmosphere loss across an inhabited zone. The urban plan is life safety architecture at civilizational scale.

**Jurisdiction complexity.** Artemis architecture explicitly includes international partners. Zoning choices that allocate access to water ice deposits, solar ridgelines, and prime construction zones are not merely technical decisions — they are de facto geopolitical ones, made in the absence of any clear jurisdictional framework.

