## 9. ETHICAL AND EXISTENTIAL STAKES

**Single-point urban failure.** Earth cities can reroute around a collapsed bridge. On the Moon, one badly positioned power line or improperly designed dust-accumulation berm can cascade to atmosphere loss across an inhabited zone. The urban plan is not infrastructure — it is life safety engineering at civilizational scale, where the margin between a bad zoning decision and a fatal one is measured in minutes.

**Precedent propagation.** The first lunar city plan becomes the template for Mars, where communication delays will range from 3 to 20 minutes. The constraints tighten rather than relax. Design errors made on the Moon are not fixed before Mars — they are inherited by it.

**International geopolitics embedded in zoning.** Artemis architecture explicitly includes international partners. Zoning decisions that allocate access to water ice deposits, solar ridge lines, and prime construction zones are simultaneously technical and geopolitical. The ethical challenge of AI in outer space has already been framed as establishing meaningful human control over the whole technological cycle and system operation [ScienceDirect] — the city plan is the physical instantiation of that principle.

**The fragmented governance landscape.** The fragmented global AI governance landscape risks exacerbating inequalities and creating regulatory arbitrage opportunities [Taylor & Francis Online, 2025]. On a lunar colony, regulatory arbitrage is not a business strategy — it is an existential risk. The city plan must be designed so that no single nation, corporation, or AI system can exploit jurisdictional gaps to gain unilateral control over life-critical infrastructure.

