## 10. WHY IT DOESN'T EXIST YET — AND WHEN IT WILL

No university today teaches this as an integrated discipline. What exists are its components in isolation: MIT's Space Architectures course proves the cross-disciplinary training model works, run jointly by Architecture, AeroAstro, and Media Lab with 35 students designing human habitation systems for space. NASA's NextSTEP-3 program is already seeking proposals on infrastructure, habitation, and concepts of operations — the job description exists in proposal form before the profession exists in faculty form. ICON's Olympus contract through 2028 means the construction tools will be operational before a single Lunar City Architect has graduated.

The profession crystallizes when three conditions converge: sustained crewed presence on the Moon (Artemis timeline places this in the early 2030s), hardware capable of autonomous city-scale construction (ICON, RASSOR-class excavation systems, multi-agent coordination algorithms), and governance frameworks that require human design responsibility for spatial decisions (the Outer Space Treaty lineage, evolving space law, and the alignment architecture research coming out of ASCEND and arXiv).

Estimated emergence as a defined professional role: **2030–2035**.

