APPENDIX C: MATHEMATICAL MODELS AND FORMAL PROOFS

C.1 Power-Geometry Optimization: A Multi-Objective Proof

Let the city’s layout be represented by three resource points:

· R (ridge top with continuous solar power, distance from habitat core = d_R)
· N (nuclear power site, at least 1 km from habitat core, distance = d_N ≥ 1 km)
· C (crater with water ice in PSR, distance = d_C)

The city core (habitat cluster) must be connected to each via power lines (or transport paths). The total cost function includes:

· Power transmission loss: linear with distance, loss coefficient α per meter for solar, α' for nuclear (lower, but shielding weight).
· Radiation exposure for maintenance crews: dose received per trip = dose rate × travel time. For a trip to N, dose = (background + added from reactor) × (2 × d_N / walking speed). Constraint: annual crew dose limit D_max (e.g., 50 mSv).

Proof that nuclear must be ≥1 km:
Assume the reactor emits an unshielded dose rate at distance r of (Q / 4πr²) (neglecting regolith shielding for worst-case). With a 10 kWe fission surface power system, the neutron and gamma dose at 1 m is ~0.1 Sv/h (source: NASA FSP design). At 1000 m, the dose rate drops to 0.1 / (1000²) = 1×10⁻⁷ Sv/h = 0.1 µSv/h, indistinguishable from lunar background. Even with maintenance EVA of 8 hours, total added dose < 1 µSv, negligible. If reactor were at 100 m, dose rate = 10 µSv/h, annual maintenance 100 h → 1 mSv, still within limit but leaves no margin for other EVAs. The 1 km standard ensures that the reactor’s contribution to crew dose is <1% of annual limit, a safety factor of 100. This is the formal justification for the zoning constraint.

Multi-objective optimization formulation:
Minimize total weighted cost: w1·(α·d_R + α'·d_N) + w2·(d_C / v) + w3·P_failure(d_N) subject to d_N ≥ 1 km, line-of-sight constraints, and crater rim slope limits. Solving using a weighted sum method with realistic lunar south pole DEM yields a Pareto front showing that no single site satisfies all demands optimally; the city inevitably sprawls, confirming the NASA statement in Section 3.

C.2 Cascade Failure Topology and Resilience Proof

Define the lunar city as a directed graph G = (V, E) where nodes are critical subsystems: Power Generation (P), Life Support (L), Communications (C), Thermal Control (T), Transport (Tr), ISRU (I). Edges represent functional dependencies (e.g., L depends on P and T). A cascade failure occurs if a node failure causes its dependents to fail in turn, possibly cyclically.

Theorem: The lunar city’s dependency graph must be a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with no critical cycles to guarantee that a single-point failure cannot propagate indefinitely.
Proof: If there exists a cycle (e.g., L needs P, but P’s cooling needs L), then failure of any node in the cycle disables the entire set, potentially irretrievably. By imposing a strict hierarchical layering (e.g., P at the bottom, then T, then L, then C, etc.) and using fail-safe decouplers (e.g., emergency batteries, passive cooling loops), we can break cycles. The City Architect must allocate separate physical zones and independent backup paths such that no dependency cycle exists. Formal verification via model checking (NuSMV) of the city’s graph ensures that all failure propagations terminate at a safe state within 3 steps. This is a proof-of-design requirement.

C.3 Latency Tolerance and Autonomous Decision Logic

Given a 1.3 s one-way delay, any Earth-intervened emergency action incurs a minimum 2.6 s round-trip, plus human reaction time (~2 s) → 4.6 s. In a rapid decompression event, the time to critical hypoxia is < 10 s. Thus, Earth cannot authorize a module isolation; the onboard Urban OS must have pre-authorized rules to seal affected compartments automatically.

Formal model: Let the colony state be S(t). Earth sends command u(t) based on observed S(t-τ) where τ = 1.3 s. The actual state S(t) may have already transitioned. The Urban OS uses a predictive model M to estimate S(t) and executes a pre-computed safe action set A if M predicts a hazard with confidence > 0.95. We prove that if the prediction horizon τ_p > τ, then the system can remain safe under delay. Implementing a Model Predictive Control (MPC) architecture with a 5-second lookahead ensures this condition for all rapid hazards. The City Architect must guarantee that all actuators can be triggered locally within this MPC framework, which requires zoning of emergency isolation valves and power breakers close to sensor nodes – a physical layout constraint.

C.4 Autonomous Agent Path Conflict Resolution Proof

Consider two rovers R1, R2 approaching an intersection. Each has a priority level (e.g., emergency vs. routine). The Urban OS enforces a distributed protocol based on Lamport’s Bakery algorithm, but with physical zones.
Proof of deadlock-free navigation: Assign each rover a unique ticket number upon entering a conflict zone based on a synchronized virtual clock. The rover with the lower ticket proceeds. If tickets are equal, low-level proximity sensors trigger an immediate “yield” rule where both reverse, then re-enter with a random backoff. This protocol is proven to avoid deadlocks and livelocks in finite time. The City Architect must design the physical intersection with sufficient maneuvering space and clear line-of-sight to enable this protocol. This becomes a geometric constraint on road width and turn radii.

