APPENDIX D: CASE STUDY — SOUTH POLE ARTEMIS BASE CAMP SPATIAL LAYOUT

D.1 Site Selection

Using LRO (Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter) illumination maps, a ridge near Shackleton Crater’s rim (89.9°S, 0°E) receives >80% sunlight annually. Within 2 km lies a PSR with up to 5 wt% water ice. A flat expanse 1.2 km down-ridge offers a safe distance for the Fission Surface Power reactor.

D.2 Zoning Plan (2035–2045)

· Zone A – Habitat Ridge: Solar arrays, pressurized habitats (initially 4 modules, expandable to 20), EVA prep rooms. All habitats connected by pressurized tunnels to avoid suit donning for daily movement, reducing suit wear and radiation exposure.
· Zone B – Nuclear Power Park: Located 1.2 km downslope, with buried power cables running along a dedicated utility corridor, marked by regolith berms for thermal and physical protection. Maintenance access via a dedicated rover road; crew EVAs only for repairs that cannot be done tele-robotically.
· Zone C – ISRU Crater Operations: A robotic mining hub within the PSR, connected by a 2.5 km sintered road to Zone A. All traffic autonomous; humans enter only in pressurized rover with radiation shielding. The road is kept shadow-free by siting on a natural ridge to avoid ice contamination.
· Zone D – Landing/Launch Complex: 5 km away on a flat plain to mitigate blast ejecta; connected by autonomous cargo transport rovers. 3D-printed blast berms surround pads per ICON designs.
· Zone E – Expansion Reserve: East of Zone A, designated for future international modules, science parks, and agriculture. Pre-zoned with utility easements.

D.3 Proof of Radiation Safety

A crew member living in Zone A (shielded habitat: 50 g/cm² regolith) receives ~50 mSv/year from GCR and solar particle events. An EVA to Zone B (1.2 km each way, speed 3 km/h) takes 48 minutes round trip, adding ~0.02 mSv from reactor (negligible). An annual 100-hour EVA budget yields <0.5 mSv reactor dose, well within limits. The layout thus meets the ALARA principle.

