APPENDIX E: GOVERNANCE AND LEGAL FRAMEWORK ANALYSIS

E.1 The Outer Space Treaty (OST) and Zoning

Article II of the OST prohibits national appropriation of celestial bodies. However, it does not address private ownership or exclusive-use zones necessary for safety. The Lunar City Architect’s zoning decisions will de facto create “safety zones” around habitats, nuclear reactors, and mining sites, which under the Artemis Accords (Section 11) are recognized as necessary and temporary. The Accords explicitly allow for “safety zones” to prevent harmful interference, and the city plan must document the size and justification of each zone based on physical necessity, not commercial monopoly. This ensures compliance with international law.

Proof of necessity: A 1 km zone around a nuclear reactor is mandated by radiation physics (Appendix C.1), not political preference. Similarly, blast exclusion zones for landing pads are based on ejecta models. The City Architect must publish these physical justifications to avoid the perception of territorial claims.

E.2 Jurisdictional Interoperability

The Artemis program is multi-national. The City Architect’s plan must provide “plug-and-play” interface standards: a 1.2 m diameter docking tunnel, 120 V DC power bus, and a standard data protocol (e.g., CCSDS). These are mandated to allow any partner module to connect without bespoke engineering. The city’s urban OS must treat all modules as equal nodes, with pre-agreed resource allocation algorithms. This avoids regulatory arbitrage, as no partner can gain unilateral control over shared life support.

