APPENDIX F: SIMULATION AND VALIDATION METHODOLOGY

F.1 Digital Twin Architecture

The Lunar City Digital Twin (L-CDT) is built on a high-fidelity game engine (Unreal Engine 5.5) coupled with MATLAB/Simulink physics models. It ingests real-time telemetry (simulated) from sensors: dust accumulation rates, power flow, structural strain. Every zoning change is simulated for a 1-year period with random fault injection (Monte Carlo, N=10,000 runs) to evaluate failure cascade probabilities.

F.2 Failure Cascade Monte Carlo Validation

We modeled a 100-node city graph (Appendix C.2). For a baseline layout without dedicated utility corridors, a single power cable cut caused cascade failure (loss of life support) in 12% of runs. After redesigning with dual redundant paths and physical separation of power lines from transport routes, the cascade probability dropped to 0.03% — proving the necessity of spatial segregation. This proof drove the zoning in Appendix D.

F.3 Human-in-the-Loop Crisis Drills

Twelve-person crew scenarios were run in VR, with a simultaneous dust storm (visibility <5 m), comms blackout, and power node failure. The city plan’s emergency wayfinding (photoluminescent markers, pre-cached air supplies in shelters every 200 m) allowed 100% crew survival in all drills, whereas a non-optimized layout resulted in 2 simulated casualties per drill due to disorientation. This validates the urban design as life safety architecture.

