APPENDIX H: ETHICAL RISK ASSESSMENT AND MITIGATION

H.1 Single-Point Urban Failure Ethics

Any city plan that contains a single point of failure for life support is ethically unacceptable. Therefore, the plan must demonstrate, through the failure cascade proof, that no one failure can cause loss of crew. The City Architect has a professional duty to refuse any layout that fails this test, analogous to a structural engineer’s refusal to certify an unsafe building.

H.2 AI Behavioral Drift Mitigation

Undetected drift in AI managing life support could gradually compromise atmosphere. The city plan must:

· Zone safety-critical AI on isolated hardware with read-only memory for core functions.
· Require periodic “digital twin cross-checks” where the twin simulates a worst-case scenario and compares the AI’s response to a physics-based baseline.
· Physically separate the safety-critical AI server room from other computing nodes, with independent power and cooling, so that a failure in non-critical AI (e.g., logistics) cannot physically damage it. This spatial isolation is an ethical requirement.

H.3 Precedent Setting for Mars

Design errors on the Moon will be inherited by Mars missions where latency is 4–22 minutes and resupply is years away. Thus, the Lunar City Architect must document all design decisions with a “Mars-Transferability Impact Statement,” ensuring that solutions scale to greater autonomy. For example, the Urban OS designed for 2.6 s round-trip must also function in degraded “Mars mode” with a 40-min delay; this requires an architecture of semi-autonomous districts that can operate independently for hours. The current city plan incorporates such district-level independence, as proven by simulation.

